  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1131 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n00z upper air analysis shows an amplified ridge over the central\nCONUS from the Gulf northward into Canada with large-scale\ntroughing on either side. An area of surface high pressure has\ndeveloped behind the eastern trough and currently occupies much\nof the Midwest and Mid-South. The high is forecast to slide\nsouthward through Sunday and would normally lead to great\nradiational cooling conditions, but increasing upper level cloud\ncoverage will manage to keep overnight cooling at bay.\nRegardless, surface dewpoints reside in the upper 20s and low\n30s, indicating our floor for low temperatures. With all of this\nin mind, both NBM and REFS data show a 60% or greater chance over\nmuch of the CWA for temperatures to drop to below freezing again\nSunday morning where lows are expected to fall to around\n30 degrees for much of the region. Some sites' temperatures have\nalready fallen close to their dewpoints prior to 06z, leading to\na question on if any fog or freezing fog potential is there\nSunday morning. Fortunately, forecast soundings show that the\nsurface moisture is likely too shallow for widespread fog. But\npatches of fog or freezing fog are possible nonetheless,\nespecially near bodies of water.\n\nThe original blocking pattern aloft will break down quickly\nthrough Sunday as the western trough escapes south, traversing\nthe southern Plains and along the Gulf shoreline, losing\namplitude with time. Once the trough reaches the Mid-South early\nMonday morning, much of the upper dynamics will have either\nshifted south or deteriorated to a moot level of strength. So,\nany upper moisture that would be advected in is not expected to\nhave the support to produce precipitation Monday. Furthermore, if\nmid-level precipitation were to be generated, evaporation due to\nhigh surface dewpoint depressions from the current dry air mass\nwill undoubtedly prevent surface precipitation.\n\nUpper ridging will return behind Monday's trough into the middle\nof this upcoming week, nixing our precipitation chances. On the\nother hand, a warming trend will start with lows climbing back\ninto the 40s by the latter half of the week. Highs will increase\nas well, reaching into the 70s. Ensembles remain in good\nagreement with one another through the end of the forecast\nperiod, indicating that troughing will largely remain west of the\nregion into next weekend, keeping the warm, dry forecast.",
